  "slug": "disorder-chern-plateaus-7d05",
  "summary": "Three recent preprints link band geometry, lattice stacking and edge effects to transport and optical selection rules in 2D platforms.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share an emphasis on geometry and symmetry controlling response in two-dimensional electronic systems, not on disorder. arxiv:2611.28213 is a tight-binding study of quantum-geometric nonlinear conductivity in SrTiO3 two-dimensional electron gases, comparing (001) and (111) interfaces, with multiorbital driving as the reported mechanism. arxiv:2611.30343 examines electronic and magnetic states in semi-Dirac quantum dots, where triangular and hexagonal geometries yield edge-asymmetric magnetism. arxiv:2611.34900 reports that rhombohedral stacking in 3R-MoS2 bilayers brightens spin- and parity-forbidden interlayer excitons, identified through g-factors and resonant hybridization. Across the set, the new element is the explicit link between geometric or stacking choices and responses that are otherwise suppressed. The methods and observables differ, so the shared thread is geometry-controlled response rather than a common mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
